rformed using a 40-step first-order ODE solver. Further implementation details and hyperparameter settings are provided in the supplementary material. 4.2 Head-to-Head Comparison on Single Rewards Table 2: Evaluations for the multi-reward setting. Gray-colored : In-domain reward. ‡ Evaluated under 1024 × 1024 1024	imes 1024 resolution. Bold : best result. Underline : second best. Model #Iter Task Metric DrawBench Metric GenEval OCR PickScore ClipScore HPSv2.1 Aesthetic ImgRwd PickScore SD-XL ‡ — 0.55 0.14 21.74 0.287 0.280 5.60 0.76 22.42 SD3.5-L ‡ — 0.71 0.68 22.24 0.289 0.288 5.50 0.96 22.91 FLUX.1-Dev — 0.66 0.59 22.55 0.295 0.274 5.71 0.96 22.84 SD3.5-M (w/o CFG) — 0.24 0.12 19.90 0.237 0.204 5.13 -0.58 20.51 + CFG — 0.63 0.59 21.75 0.285 0.279 5.36 0.85 22.34 + FlowGRPO > > 5k 0.95 0.66 21.67 0.293 0.274 5.32 1.06 22.37 2k 0.66 0.92 21.80 0.290 0.280 5.32 0.95 22.44 4k 0.54 0.68 23.33 0.280 0.316 5.90 1.29 23.50 + DiffusionNFT 1.7k 0.93 0.90 23.36 0.293 0.330 5.99 1.48 23.79 + Ours 1.5k 0.93 0.91 23.60 0.296 0.341 6.04 1.51 23.91 Figure 3: Qualitative Comparison. The text prompts are sampled from GenEval, OCR, PickScore, and DrawBench evaluations, illustrating the generation fidelity of our FlowAWR. We first compare FlowAWR with DiffusionNFT under the single-reward setting. As shown in Table 1 and Figure 2 , FlowAWR achieves a 2 × 2	imes to 5 × 5	imes acceleration in training iterations. For the OCR task, the Figure 2(c) illustrates the baseline performance of DiffusionNFT without its extreme setting of β = 0.1  eta=0.1 (which corresponds to an aggressive hard-clipped advantage of 𝒜 NFT = ± 10 \mathcal{A}_{	ext{NFT}}=\pm 10 ), further indicating that its efficacy depends heavily on heuristic tuning. As evidenced in Table 1 , FlowAWR exhibits reduced reward hacking during out-of-domain evaluations. However, exclusively optimizing a single rule-based objective (e.g., GenEval or OCR) typically degrades overall image aesthetics due to the absence of holistic visual